{
  "dom_id": "step-q38218",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "Let $f(x)$ be the function defined below. Find the value of $a,$ if any, for which $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x-2}, & x<2 \\\\ ax, & x\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "From the definition of $f(x),$ the left-sided limit of $f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}\\frac{1}{x-2}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{+}}ax=2a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to 2}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ there is no value of $a$ that makes $f(x)$ continuous at $x=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
